    "explanation": "Variant C's superiority rests on four interlocking design decisions: (1) Decomposition — each of the 8 requirements is assessed independently, preventing one complex clause from dominating the overall assessment; (2) Explicit per-requirement criteria — pass/fail is defined for each requirement, not inferred from vague policy language; (3) Evidence anchoring — verbatim quotes tether each finding to specific contract text, enabling auditor verification; (4) Confidence thresholds with a deterministic aggregation rule — the < 0.75 flag transforms calibrated uncertainty into a machine-readable signal and defines exactly when the overall contract is flagged. Variants A and B lack decomposition, evidence, and confidence management. Variant C is not merely longer — its length encodes a complete, auditable methodology.",
    "whyWrong": {
      "A": "Length alone does not improve accuracy; more context is valuable only when it encodes relevant constraints. A longer prompt with vague criteria would still produce inconsistent results.",
      "C": "Evidence quotes reduce hallucination risk and improve auditability but do not eliminate false positives; confidence thresholds and explicit per-requirement criteria are equally important components of false positive management.",
      "D": "Claude does not hallucinate policy requirements when given a vague prompt — it applies its own interpretation of what constitutes a violation, which is a consistency problem, not a hallucination problem per se. Variant C solves the consistency problem by explicitly defining each requirement."
    },
