    "explanation": "When all few-shot examples show the same output class (here: 'no issues'), Claude learns a strong prior toward that output. For tasks where identifying deviations (bugs, violations, anomalies) is the primary value, the few-shot set must include negative examples — cases where the correct action is to flag a problem — so Claude learns both the positive (LGTM) and negative (bug found) response patterns.",
