    "type": "single",
    "select": 1,
    "question": "Which consensus mechanism is the team using, and what is its key advantage in this scenario?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Unanimous agreement; it ensures no false positives reach the engineering team",
      "B": "Majority vote; it surfaces the issue despite one reviewer missing it, reducing the impact of any single reviewer's blind spot",
      "C": "Structured disagreement; it forces a fourth review instance to resolve the tie",
      "D": "Weighted confidence scoring; it discards the outlier instance's response entirely"
    },
    "correct": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanation": "Majority vote (2 of 3 reviewers flag the issue) correctly identifies the authentication problem even though one reviewer missed it. The advantage is resilience: a single reviewer's blind spot or reasoning failure does not suppress a valid finding. This is the canonical use case for majority voting in multi-instance review.",
    "whyWrong": {
      "A": "Unanimous agreement would require all three instances to agree; with one approving and two flagging, unanimous consensus would suppress the valid finding — the opposite of what is needed.",
      "C": "Structured disagreement is a mechanism for surfacing and articulating differences, not a tie-breaker for a 2-vs-1 split; a fourth instance is not typically summoned by majority vote.",
      "D": "Discarding the outlier entirely without examination removes potentially useful signal; majority vote acts on the outcome without discarding any reviewer's full response."
    },
    "refs": [
      "Claude Certified Architect Study Guide, Domain 4, Section 4.6 – Consensus mechanisms: majority vote",
      "Anthropic multi-agent orchestration patterns"
    ],
