    "source": "Connectry Labs",
    "id": "C-q-4.6-hard-1",
    "scenario": "A team is building a multi-pass review system for a distributed system's architecture document. Phase 1 uses three independent instances to review individual component designs. Phase 2 receives Phase 1 summaries and performs cross-component consistency analysis. However, the team notices that Phase 2 is consistently reproducing the same blind spots as Phase 1 because Phase 2 is seeded with Phase 1 summaries.",
    "domain": "Prompt Engineering & Structured Output",
    "task": "4.6",
    "taskTitle": "Design multi-instance and multi-pass review architectures",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "type": "single",
    "select": 1,
    "question": "What architectural change would most effectively address this bias propagation problem?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Increase the number of Phase 1 instances from three to five",
      "B": "Replace Phase 1 summaries with structured finding schemas that separate conclusions from raw observations, and have Phase 2 re-examine the original artifact independently before consulting Phase 1 outputs",
      "C": "Run Phase 2 before Phase 1 to avoid being anchored on Phase 1 findings",
      "D": "Use a different LLM provider for Phase 2 to ensure model diversity"
    },
    "correct": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanation": "When Phase 2 is seeded with Phase 1 summaries, it inherits Phase 1's framing and blind spots — a form of anchoring bias propagation. The fix has two parts: (1) separate raw observations from conclusions in Phase 1 output so Phase 2 can reason from evidence rather than inherited verdicts, and (2) have Phase 2 independently examine the original artifact before consulting Phase 1 findings. This preserves Phase 2's independence while still allowing it to benefit from Phase 1 signal.",
    "whyWrong": {
      "A": "Adding more Phase 1 instances increases coverage of Phase 1's perspective but does not address the structural problem that Phase 2 is anchored on Phase 1 summaries; the bias propagation path remains intact.",
      "C": "Running Phase 2 before Phase 1 would make Phase 2 lack the benefit of per-component analysis, and would not fix the eventual anchoring if Phase 1 results were later fed in.",
      "D": "Model diversity can reduce correlated errors but does not address the structural anchoring caused by seeding Phase 2 with Phase 1 conclusions; the propagation path must be architecturally broken."
    },
    "refs": [
      "Claude Certified Architect Study Guide, Domain 4, Section 4.6 – Independent review instances",
      "Claude Certified Architect Study Guide, Domain 4, Section 4.6 – Per-file and cross-file passes"
    ],
