    "source": "Connectry Labs",
    "id": "C-q-4.6-hard-2",
    "scenario": "A healthcare AI company uses a five-instance majority-vote review for clinical decision support outputs. An internal audit reveals that all five instances share the same systematic bias toward recommending a particular drug class due to training data skew. All five agree on every review, giving the team false confidence in the consensus.",
    "domain": "Prompt Engineering & Structured Output",
    "task": "4.6",
    "taskTitle": "Design multi-instance and multi-pass review architectures",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "type": "single",
    "select": 1,
    "question": "What does this scenario expose as a fundamental limitation of majority vote, and what architectural strategy best mitigates it?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Majority vote cannot handle more than three instances; reducing to three instances would fix the false confidence",
      "B": "Correlated errors across instances mean unanimous agreement does not imply correctness; mitigation requires either using models with architecturally different training or injecting adversarial reviewer instances with explicit counter-hypothesis prompts",
      "C": "The solution is to add a sixth instance to break potential ties",
      "D": "Majority vote should be replaced with confidence threshold filtering: only accept findings above 0.9 confidence"
    },
    "correct": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanation": "Majority vote assumes that errors are uncorrelated across instances. When all instances share the same systematic bias (e.g., from identical training data), they produce correlated errors and unanimity provides no quality signal — it is merely five echoes of the same flaw. Mitigation requires breaking correlation: using models trained on different data distributions, or injecting adversarial reviewer instances explicitly prompted to argue the counter-hypothesis, forces genuine disagreement that can surface the blind spot.",
    "whyWrong": {
      "A": "The number of instances is irrelevant to correlated bias; reducing from five to three would not change the underlying systematic error if all instances share it.",
      "C": "A sixth instance with the same bias would only reinforce the false consensus; the problem is correlation, not count.",
      "D": "Confidence thresholds filter low-confidence responses but do not address systematic bias; a biased instance can produce high-confidence wrong answers, making confidence filtering ineffective against correlated errors."
    },
    "refs": [
      "Claude Certified Architect Study Guide, Domain 4, Section 4.6 – Consensus mechanisms: majority vote",
      "Anthropic documentation: Correlated failures in multi-agent systems"
    ],
