    "id": "C-q-5.1-medium-1",
    "scenario": "A team runs a multi-session research assistant. After each session ends, the assistant produces a progressive summary of findings. In the third session, the team notices the assistant has forgotten a key constraint from session one that was only mentioned briefly in session one's summary.",
    "domain": "Context Management & Reliability",
    "task": "5.1",
    "taskTitle": "Manage conversation context to preserve critical information",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "type": "single",
    "select": 1,
    "question": "Which limitation of progressive summarization most likely caused this information loss?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Progressive summaries compress content each round, and details not reinforced in later sessions gradually drop out of the summary.",
      "B": "The summarization prompt used a temperature of 0, which prevents creative retention of edge-case details.",
      "C": "Claude's summarization always prioritizes the most recent turn over all previous turns.",
      "D": "Session boundaries automatically reset all prior constraints regardless of the summary strategy used."
    },
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "Progressive summarization involves compressing an already-compressed summary each session. Each compression round introduces information loss, and details that appear only briefly — especially constraints or edge cases — are disproportionately likely to be dropped when the model decides what is most salient. Critical constraints must be explicitly pinned or re-injected rather than relying on progressive compression to preserve them.",
    "whyWrong": {
      "B": "Temperature controls output randomness for generation tasks; it does not affect what semantic content a summarization model decides to retain or discard.",
      "C": "Claude does not have a hard-coded recency bias rule that always deprioritizes earlier content; the issue is the cumulative information loss inherent in repeated summarization.",
      "D": "Session boundaries are an application-layer concept; Claude itself has no mechanism that automatically resets constraints. The information loss is due to summarization compression, not a session reset."
    },
    "refs": [
      "https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/conversation-management",
      "https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/context-windows"
    ],
